  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8x+3}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8y+3}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8y+3}}+2 \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8y+3}} \\\\ \\sqrt{8y+3} & =\\frac{k}{x-2} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{8y+3})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{k}{x-2})}^{2} \\\\ 8y+3 & =\\frac{{k}^{2}}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ 8y & =\\frac{{k}^{2}}{(x-2)^{2}}-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{k}^{2}}{8(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{k}^{2}}{8(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{3}{8},\\,x>2.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{k}^{2}}{8}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=6.\n$$",
